On Mon, 2011-08-15, Jukka Lahtinen wrote:
> I updated the dump and paranoid patches linked to my Nethack page at
> http://www.netsonic.fi/~walker/nethack.html
>
> The newest version of the dump patch now has support for macro %d in the
> filename. When the file is written, %d is replaced by the current date
> and time in format YYYYMMDDHHMMSS.
> So, if the filename is defined like
> OPTIONS=dumpfile:/tmp/%n-%d.nh
> and your character called Foo dies on the 15th of August 2011 at
> 16:44:15, the actual filename will be /tmp/Foo-20110815164415.nh.

Pet peeve: I really dislike that date format.

/tmp/Foo-20110815164415.nh  # original
/tmp/Foo-201108151644.nh    # no need for sub-minute resolution[1]
/tmp/Foo-20110815_1644.nh   # split date from time for readability
/tmp/Foo-2011-08-15_1644.nh # split yyyy-mm-dd for readability

In this case it's just a minor complaint -- it's just that I spend my
days handling a *lot* of timestamps like that. You never get used to
them.

/Jorgen

[1] For bonus points you could append the 'seconds' part if and only
    if you try to save and find that the file already exists.

